{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## What\n\nCut the emit-side surfaces over to `[non-codebase]`, teaching the recognition-side surfaces to\naccept the legacy spelling without documenting it.\n\nCUTOVER (emit): `plan_review_E2.md:12`, `plan_review_E6.md:12`, `plan_review_F1.md:10`,\n`coach_moves.py` move 14 (comment + template).\nCOMPATIBILITY (recognize both): `completion_verifier.md`, `plan_review_evidence_kind.md`,\n`plan_review_ac_process_gate.md`, `.rebar/prompts/plan-review-project-measurement-provenance.md`\n(its SCOPE clause gates whether the criterion applies at all), the eval-spec fixtures.\n\n## Verbatim quoters of the move-14 template\n\n`coach_moves.py` move 14's template string is reproduced VERBATIM in two other places. Changing\nthe template without them leaves the docs misquoting real coach output:\n\n- `docs/plan-review-gate.md:335` — the coach-move table row. IN SCOPE for this story. (No\n  automated byte-identity gate enforces this row — the nearby guide parity test covers overlay\n  ranges, not the move table — so it is a doc-accuracy defect rather than a CI break, and it is\n  fixed here because this story owns the template.)\n- `src/rebar/_guides/writing-a-passing-plan.md:204` — OUT OF SCOPE, owned by S5, which also\n  flips the coupled assertion in `tests/unit/test_guides.py:76-82`. S5 `depends_on` this story,\n  so the two land in order and the whole stack lands together; the guide's quote is stale only\n  within the stack, never on `main`.\n\n## Boundary with S7 in `completion_verifier.md`\n\nBoth stories edit that file and S7 `depends_on` this one, so the edits are ordered. The split is\nby concern: THIS story makes the file RECOGNIZE both TAG spellings. The `kind` WIRE-VALUE emit\ncutover ADR 0101 requires \"in lockstep\" — including `completion_verifier.md:224`\n(`kind`: `codebase-verifiable` or `operator-attested`) — is explicitly S7's, alongside\n`contracts.py` and the JSON schema, so the emitted enum changes in ONE place rather than being\nsplit across two changes.\n\n## Why this is ONE commit\n\nThree gates are coupled to this text and all fail if split:\n1. `tests/unit/test_measurement_provenance_criterion.py` pins SHA256 digests AND exact line\n   counts for five files. Its `_contract_lines()` selector keeps lines containing\n   `\"OPERATOR-ATTESTED RULE\"` or `\"[operator-attested]\"` — cut over without widening the\n   SELECTOR and it matches ZERO lines, failing with a misleading\n   \"expected 1 contract line(s), found 0\".\n2. `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` is generated from these prompts and gated by\n   `registry.validate_criteria_guide(...) == []` in five test files plus content pins in\n   `tests/unit/test_generated_surface_markers.py`.\n3. The `reviewers/index.json` drift gate fires on any front-matter change.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Emit-side prompts and coach move 14 instruct `[non-codebase]`.\n- [ ] Recognition-side prompts state that the legacy spelling is still accepted.\n- [ ] `_contract_lines()` selects the new text; digests and counts are recomputed in this commit.\n- [ ] `python -m rebar.llm.plan_review.registry regenerate-criteria-guide` run and committed;\n      `validate_criteria_guide` returns `[]`.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787197662469508001,"uuid":"9a6e2191-6d00-4798-bf34-2092e3553d2d"}